Helios DXVK fork: D3D11 video decoder (D3D11VA, H.264) on Vulkan Video

Conduit guest/windows/docs/d3d11-video-decode.md. Applied to third_party/dxvk
by ci/windows/Build-Driver.ps1 after 0001 (the fork is not ours to push to).

- ID3D11VideoDevice: one decoder profile, D3D11_DECODER_PROFILE_H264_VLD_NOFGT
  with NV12 output, configs ConfigBitstreamRaw 2 (short slices) and 1 (long);
  NV12 reports D3D11_FORMAT_SUPPORT_DECODER_OUTPUT. Only when the Vulkan
  device has VK_KHR_video_queue/_decode_queue/_decode_h264 and a decode queue
  family (DXVK_HELIOS_VIDEO_DECODE=0 turns it off).
- ID3D11VideoDecoder / ID3D11VideoDecoderOutputView and the decoder entry
  points of ID3D11VideoContext (GetDecoderBuffer, DecoderBeginFrame,
  SubmitDecoderBuffers, DecoderEndFrame). DXVA_PicParams_H264 and
  DXVA_Qmatrix_H264 become StdVideo SPS/PPS (ids 0, new session parameters
  when they change), slice control becomes the slice offsets, RefFrameList
  becomes DPB slots (a surface holds a slot while it is referenced). Output
  and DPB coincide: the picture is decoded into the app's NV12 texture layer.
- D3D11_BIND_DECODER NV12 textures get VIDEO_DECODE_DST|DPB usage with the
  H.264 profile list, CONCURRENT sharing with the decode queue family, and are
  never relocated.
- Decode runs on its own queue. Per frame the CS thread brings the touched
  layers into their default layout (DxvkContext::prepareImageForExternalQueue),
  signals a fence, flushes, submits the decode waiting on that fence and
  signalling the decode fence, and makes the next graphics submission wait
  for it. The bitstream is written by the app straight into a host-visible
  ring of 8 slots.
- Video processor: studio-range input now also expands chroma (it only
  expanded luma, so colors came out desaturated), and Nominal_Range 0-255 is
  no longer treated as studio range.

